  p:["Skipped or fluttering beats are usually harmless and are often triggered by caffeine, stress, alcohol or short sleep."],
  b:["See a doctor if it happens often, lasts more than a few minutes or comes with chest pain, breathlessness, dizziness or fainting.","This sensor cannot diagnose rhythm problems. Only an ECG can."],rel:['tachy','caffeine']},
 {id:'maxhr',q:"What is my max heart rate?",kw:['max heart rate','maximum heart rate','heart rate zone','heart rate zones','target heart rate','heart rate during exercise','exercise heart rate','heart rate while running','heart rate when running'],
  p:["A common rough estimate of maximum heart rate is 220 minus your age. It varies a lot between people."],
  b:["Moderate exercise is usually about 50 to 70% of your maximum.","Vigorous exercise is about 70 to 85%.","If you have a heart condition or take heart medicines, ask your doctor for your own limits.","Tell me your age, for example \"max heart rate for age 35\", and I will work out your zones."],rel:['exercise','hrrec']},
 {id:'hrrec',q:"How fast should my heart rate recover after exercise?",kw:['heart rate recovery','heart rate recover','recover after exercise','recovery after exercise','cool down','cooldown'],
  p:["After exercise your pulse should drop steadily. A fall of more than about 12 beats in the first minute is generally seen as a good sign."],
  b:["Slower recovery can mean low fitness, tiredness, dehydration or illness.","Track the same test over weeks. Your trend matters more than one result."],rel:['maxhr','exercise']},
 {id:'lowspo2',q:"What causes low oxygen levels?",kw:['oxygen 94','oxygen 93','oxygen 92','oxygen 91','oxygen 90','spo2 94','spo2 93','spo2 92','spo2 91','spo2 90','my oxygen is low','low oxygen','low spo2','oxygen level low','oxygen levels low','hypoxia','oxygen dropping','spo2 low','oxygen too low','spo2 dropping','oxygen saturation low'],v:'spo2',
  p:["A reading under 95% can come from real causes such as lung problems, infections, sleep apnea or high altitude. It can also be a measurement problem such as cold fingers, nail polish, movement or a loose fit."],
  b:["Warm your hands, remove nail polish, sit still and measure again.","If it stays under 95%, speak to a doctor. Under 92%, or with breathlessness, get medical help."],rel:['spoacc','altitude']},
 {id:'spoacc',q:"How accurate is the SpO2 reading?",kw:['how accurate','accurate','accuracy','nail polish','skin tone','dark skin','darker skin','wrong reading','inaccurate','reliable','trust reading'],
  p:["Finger sensors give a useful estimate, not a medical-grade measurement. PulseSpace uses an uncalibrated calculation, so treat SpO₂ as a trend."],
  b:["Movement, cold fingers, nail polish, bright light and pressing too hard can all throw it off.","Studies show pulse oximeters can read higher than the true value on darker skin, so do not rely on a normal number if you feel unwell.","Carbon monoxide from smoking can also make the reading look normal."],rel:['lowspo2','meddevice']},
 {id:'altitude',q:"Does altitude affect SpO2?",kw:['altitude','high altitude','mountain','trek','trekking','hill station'],
  p:["Oxygen levels can sit below 95% at high altitude, and that is often expected while your body adjusts."],
  b:["Rest, drink water and go up gradually.","Severe headache, confusion, breathlessness at rest or a bluish tint need urgent care and a move to lower ground."],rel:['lowspo2']},
 {id:'apnea',q:"What is sleep apnea?",kw:['sleep apnea','apnea','apnoea','snoring','snore','oxygen at night','oxygen during sleep'],
  p:["Sleep apnea means breathing repeatedly pauses during sleep, causing oxygen dips. Loud snoring, gasping at night, morning headaches and daytime sleepiness are common signs."],
  b:["A short finger check while awake cannot detect it.","If this sounds familiar, ask a doctor about a sleep study."],rel:['sleep','lowspo2']},
 {id:'bp',q:"Can PulseSpace measure blood pressure?",kw:['blood pressure','hypertension','high bp','low bp','hypotension','bp'],
  p:["No. PulseSpace cannot measure blood pressure. Use a validated upper-arm cuff monitor, seated and rested."],
  b:["Under about 120/80 mmHg is generally considered normal.","Repeated readings of 140/90 or higher are worth a doctor visit.","A very high reading such as 180/120 with chest pain, headache or confusion is an emergency."],rel:['hearthealth']},
 {id:'bodytemp',q:"Is the sensor temperature my body temperature?",kw:['body temperature','fever','thermometer','normal temperature','high temperature','body temp'],
  p:["No. The temperature on PulseSpace is the sensor chip's own temperature, so it cannot detect a fever."],
  b:["Use a digital thermometer. A fever is generally 38 °C (100.4 °F) or higher.","See a doctor for a high or long-lasting fever, or any fever with stiff neck, confusion or trouble breathing."],rel:['illness']},
 {id:'hrvgood',q:"What is a good HRV?",kw:['good hrv','normal hrv','low hrv','high hrv','hrv range','hrv by age','good rmssd','normal rmssd','low rmssd','hrv drop','hrv score'],v:'rmssd',
  p:["There is no single good number. HRV (RMSSD) is very personal and falls with age. Many healthy adults sit somewhere between 20 and 70 ms."],
  b:["PulseSpace treats 40 ms or more as strong recovery and under 20 ms as low.","Compare with your own average. A drop lasting several days often points to stress, poor sleep or illness."],rel:['sleep','mindful']},
 {id:'sleep',q:"How does sleep affect my heart rate?",kw:['sleep','sleeping','poor sleep','bedtime','sleepless'],
  p:["Your heart rate is lowest during sleep. Short or broken sleep raises resting heart rate and lowers HRV the next day."],
  b:["Aim for seven to nine hours on a regular schedule.","Keep the room dark and cool, and avoid screens before bed.","Avoid caffeine in the afternoon and heavy meals or alcohol close to bedtime."],rel:['caffeine','alcohol']},
 {id:'caffeine',q:"How does caffeine affect my readings?",kw:['caffeine','coffee','tea','energy drink','energy drinks','chai'],
  p:["Caffeine can raise heart rate and lower HRV for three to six hours."],
  b:["Measure before your first cup, or wait two to three hours after.","Up to about 400 mg a day, roughly four cups of coffee, is considered fine for most adults."],rel:['sleep','tachy']},
 {id:'alcohol',q:"How does alcohol affect HRV?",kw:['alcohol','beer','wine','hangover','whisky','liquor'],
  p:["Alcohol raises resting heart rate and lowers HRV, and the effect can last into the next morning."],
  b:["Expect a poorer recovery score the day after drinking.","Less alcohol, and finishing earlier in the evening, helps both sleep and HRV."],rel:['sleep','hrvgood']},
 {id:'smoking',q:"Does smoking change my heart rate and SpO2?",kw:['smoking','smoke','cigarette','nicotine','vaping','vape','tobacco','beedi'],
  p:["Nicotine raises heart rate and tightens blood vessels."],
  b:["Carbon monoxide from smoke can make a pulse oximeter show a normal oxygen level even when your blood is carrying less.","Quitting improves heart rate, HRV and oxygen delivery within weeks. A doctor or quit-line can help."],rel:['spoacc','hearthealth']},
 {id:'exercise',q:"How much exercise should I do?",kw:['exercise','workout','cardio','gym','how much exercise','best exercise','jogging','running','walking','jog'],
  p:["Adults are advised to get about 150 minutes of moderate activity a week, such as brisk walking, plus two days of strength work."],
  b:["Regular exercise lowers resting heart rate and raises HRV over weeks.","Start small and build up. Measure at rest, not straight after a workout."],rel:['maxhr','hrrec']},
 {id:'water',q:"How much water should I drink?",kw:['water','hydration','dehydrated','dehydration','how much water','thirsty','fluids','hydrate'],
  p:["There is no single amount for everyone. Drink enough through the day that your urine is pale yellow."],
  b:["Dehydration raises heart rate and can lower HRV.","Drink more in heat and when exercising."],rel:['tachy','caffeine']},
 {id:'diet',q:"What foods are good for my heart?",kw:['diet','food','what to eat','meal','mediterranean','healthy eating','nutrition'],
  p:["A heart-friendly pattern is mostly vegetables, fruit, legumes, whole grains, nuts, fish and healthy oils, with less salt, sugar and ultra-processed food."],
  b:["Large meals can lift your pulse for an hour, so measure before eating or wait a while after.","Regular meal times help keep energy and stress steadier."],rel:['hearthealth','water']},
 {id:'stressmgmt',q:"How do I manage stress long term?",kw:['manage stress','stress management','long term stress','anxiety','anxious','overwhelmed','burnout','mental health'],v:'stress',
  p:["Long-term stress responds best to steady habits."],
  b:["Sleep regularly, move daily and spend some time outdoors.","Schedule breaks, limit news and screens, and stay in touch with people you trust.","Practice slow breathing for a few minutes each day.","If worry or low mood lasts for weeks or affects daily life, talk to a doctor or counsellor."],w:true,rel:['breathtech','mindful']},
 {id:'mindful',q:"Does yoga or meditation help HRV?",kw:['yoga','mindfulness','meditation','meditate'],
  p:["Yes. Regular practice tends to raise HRV and lower resting heart rate, and even ten minutes a day helps."],
  b:["Slow breathing is the part that works fastest.","Try the breathing pacer below, then measure again to see the change."],w:true,rel:['breathtech','hrvgood']},
 {id:'breathtech',q:"What is box breathing?",kw:['box breathing','4 7 8','478 breathing','diaphragmatic','belly breathing','breathing technique','breathing techniques','breathing exercise','breathing exercises','paced breathing','slow breathing','pranayama'],
  p:["Slow breathing calms your nervous system and raises HRV. A few easy options:"],
  b:["Paced breathing: in for 4 seconds, out for 6. This is what the pacer uses.","Box breathing: in 4, hold 4, out 4, hold 4.","4-7-8: in 4, hold 7, out 8.","Belly breathing: breathe low so your stomach rises, not your chest.","Stop if you feel lightheaded and breathe normally."],w:true,rel:['mindful','stressmgmt']},
 {id:'coldhands',q:"Why are my fingers cold and the signal weak?",kw:['cold hands','cold fingers','poor circulation','raynaud','fingers cold','hands cold','cold finger'],
