  b:["Visit a dentist every six to twelve months.","A toothache with swelling or fever needs prompt dental care."],rel:['diet']},
 {id:'immunity',q:"How can I boost my immunity?",kw:['immunity','immune system','boost immunity','immune','immunize'],
  p:["There is no single supplement for immunity. Regular sleep, a balanced diet, daily activity, up-to-date vaccines, hand washing, not smoking and managing stress all help."],
  b:["Be wary of products that promise to boost immunity."],rel:['sleep','vaccines']},
 {id:'vaccines',q:"Which vaccines should adults get?",kw:['vaccine','vaccines','vaccination','vaccinated','immunization','flu shot','booster','jab'],
  p:["It depends on your country, age and health. Commonly recommended are a yearly flu shot, a tetanus booster every 10 years, COVID-19 vaccination as advised locally, and extra vaccines for older adults or people with long-term conditions."],
  b:["Ask your doctor or local health department for the schedule that fits you."],rel:['immunity']},
 {id:'firstaid',q:"What is basic first aid for a minor burn?",kw:['first aid','minor burn','burn injury','scald','minor cut','nosebleed','nose bleed','sprain','sprained ankle','wound care','insect bite','bee sting'],
  p:["A few basics:"],
  b:["Burn: cool it under running water for 20 minutes. No ice, butter or toothpaste. Cover loosely.","Cut: press with a clean cloth, rinse, then bandage.","Sprain: rest, ice for 15 to 20 minutes, a snug bandage and keep the limb raised.","Nosebleed: sit up, lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes.","Get medical help for deep or large wounds, burns on the face, hands or large areas, or bleeding that will not stop after 10 minutes of pressure."],rel:['cpr']},
 {id:'cpr',q:"How do I do CPR?",kw:['cpr','choking','heimlich','cardiac arrest','resuscitation','unconscious','not breathing'],
  p:["Call your local emergency number first, or ask someone nearby to call. Then, for an adult who is unresponsive and not breathing normally:"],
  b:["Push hard and fast in the center of the chest, about 100 to 120 times a minute and 5 to 6 cm (2 inches) deep, letting the chest rise fully each time.","If you are untrained, hands-only CPR is fine. A trained person can add 2 breaths after every 30 pushes.","Use an AED (defibrillator) as soon as one arrives and follow its voice prompts.","Choking adult who cannot cough, speak or breathe: give 5 back blows, then 5 abdominal thrusts, and repeat until it clears or help arrives."],
  after:"A certified first-aid course gives you hands-on practice and is well worth taking.",rel:['emsigns']},
 {id:'emsigns',q:"What are the warning signs of a heart attack or stroke?",kw:['heart attack signs','heart attack symptoms','signs of heart attack','stroke signs','stroke symptoms','warning signs','heart attack','fast test'],
  p:["Call your local emergency number immediately if you notice these signs. Do not wait for them to pass and do not drive yourself."],
  b:["Heart attack: pressure, squeezing or pain in the chest that may spread to the arm, jaw, neck or back, with breathlessness, cold sweat, nausea or light-headedness. Women may have milder signs such as unusual tiredness or back or jaw pain.","Stroke (think FAST): Face drooping, Arm or leg weakness, Speech difficulty, Time to call emergency now. Sudden severe headache, vision loss or loss of balance also count."],rel:['cpr']},
 {id:'heat',q:"How do I avoid heat exhaustion?",kw:['heat stroke','heatstroke','heat exhaustion','sunstroke','hot weather','summer heat','heat wave'],
  p:["In hot weather drink water often (add ORS or salt-and-sugar fluids if you sweat a lot), avoid midday sun and wear light, loose clothing."],
  b:["Heat exhaustion: heavy sweating, weakness, dizziness, headache or nausea. Move to a cool place, loosen clothes and sip fluids.","Heatstroke: confusion, hot dry skin, a temperature above about 40 °C or fainting. This is an emergency, so call for help and cool the person while waiting."],rel:['water','dizzy']},
 {id:'weightloss',q:"How can I lose weight safely?",kw:['lose weight','weight loss','fat loss','belly fat','obesity','overweight','gain weight','weight gain','reduce weight'],
  p:["A safe pace is about 0.25 to 0.5 kg (0.5 to 1 lb) a week, from a modest calorie deficit that you can keep up."],
  b:["Eat more protein, fibre and vegetables, and fewer sugary drinks and snacks.","Walk daily and add strength training twice a week.","Sleep well. Short sleep drives hunger.","Avoid crash diets. Ask a doctor first if you have a medical condition."],after:"Tell me your weight and height, for example \"BMI for 70 kg 170 cm\", and I will calculate your BMI.",rel:['bmi','balanced']},
 {id:'bmi',q:"What is BMI?",kw:['bmi','body mass index','healthy weight','ideal weight'],
  p:["BMI is weight in kilograms divided by height in metres squared. For adults, 18.5 to 24.9 is generally the healthy range, 25 to 29.9 is overweight and 30 or more is obese."],
  b:["Many guidelines use lower cut-offs for South Asian adults, around 23 for overweight.","BMI ignores muscle and body shape, so it is a rough guide only.","Ask me for example \"BMI for 70 kg 170 cm\" and I will calculate it."],rel:['weightloss']},
 {id:'protein',q:"How much protein do I need?",kw:['protein','whey','protein powder','build muscle','muscle gain','amino acids'],
  p:["Most adults need about 0.8 g of protein per kg of body weight a day. Active people often do well with 1.2 to 2 g per kg."],
  b:["Good sources are dal and lentils, eggs, paneer, curd, fish, chicken, tofu and nuts.","If you have kidney disease, ask your doctor before raising protein."],rel:['strength','diet']},
 {id:'vitamins',q:"Do I need vitamin supplements?",kw:['vitamin','vitamins','supplement','supplements','multivitamin','vitamin d','vitamin c','vitamin b12','b12','omega 3','fish oil','magnesium','zinc','iron tablets'],
  p:["Most people get what they need from a varied diet. Vitamin D and B12 are the common shortfalls, especially with little sun or a vegetarian or vegan diet."],
  b:["A blood test can confirm a shortfall.","More is not better, and some supplements interact with medicines. Tell your doctor what you take."],rel:['anemia','diet']},
 {id:'fasting',q:"Is intermittent fasting safe?",kw:['intermittent fasting','fasting','keto','crash diet','detox','fad diet','juice cleanse'],
  p:["It can work for some healthy adults, but it is not magic. What matters is a pattern you can keep up."],
  b:["Avoid it in pregnancy, with diabetes medicines, with an eating disorder history and under 18 unless your doctor agrees.","Be wary of detoxes, crash diets and cleanses."],rel:['balanced','weightloss']},
 {id:'balanced',q:"What is a balanced diet?",kw:['how many calories','calorie intake','daily calories','calories per day','balanced diet','healthy diet','portion','calories','calorie','plate method','meal plan','diet plan'],
  p:["A simple plate: half vegetables and fruit, a quarter whole grains, a quarter protein, plus a little healthy fat."],
  b:["Limit sugary drinks, deep-fried food and ultra-processed snacks.","Calorie needs vary, often around 1,800 to 2,500 a day for adults depending on size and activity."],rel:['protein','weightloss']},
 {id:'insomnia',q:"How can I fall asleep faster?",kw:['sleep early','sleep faster','sleep on time','fall asleep','trouble sleeping','difficulty sleeping','sleep problems','wake up at night','waking up','sleep better','insomnia','cant sleep','sleepless nights'],
  p:["A steady routine helps most."],
  b:["Wake at the same time daily and get morning daylight.","Dim lights and avoid screens for an hour before bed. Keep the room cool and dark.","No caffeine for eight hours before bed.","If you are awake for 20 minutes, get up, do something calm and try again. Slow breathing helps.","See a doctor if poor sleep lasts more than three months, or you snore loudly or gasp at night."],rel:['sleep','breathtech']},
 {id:'posture',q:"How do I fix my posture after sitting all day?",kw:['posture','sitting too long','sitting all day','desk job','sedentary','standing desk','stretching','stretch','ergonomic'],
  p:["Long sitting stiffens the back and raises health risks. Stand up and move for a few minutes every 30 to 60 minutes."],
  b:["Keep your screen at eye level, feet flat and back supported.","Stretch your hips, chest and shoulders, and walk during calls."],rel:['backpain','steps']},
 {id:'steps',q:"How many steps should I walk a day?",kw:['steps a day','how many steps','10000 steps','step count','daily steps','steps per day','walk daily'],
  p:["Research suggests most of the benefit comes between about 7,000 and 10,000 steps a day, and every extra thousand helps."],
  b:["If you are starting from a low number, add 1,000 steps a week.","A brisk walk of 30 minutes is roughly 3,000 to 4,000 steps."],rel:['exercise','strength']},
 {id:'strength',q:"Should I do strength training?",kw:['strength training','weights','weight training','lifting','muscle strength','resistance training','push ups','pushups','dumbbell'],
  p:["Yes. Two days a week working all the main muscle groups improves strength, bone density, blood sugar and blood pressure."],
  b:["Start light, learn good form and increase slowly.","Body-weight moves such as squats, push-ups and planks count."],rel:['protein','exercise']},
 {id:'running',q:"How do I start running as a beginner?",kw:['start running','beginner running','running beginner','couch to 5k','5k','10k','marathon'],
  p:["Begin with run-walk intervals, for example one minute running and two minutes walking, three days a week."],
  b:["Add no more than about 10% to your weekly time or distance.","Wear proper shoes and rest between sessions. Pain that worsens means stop."],rel:['maxhr','hrrec']},
 {id:'menstrual',q:"Does my menstrual cycle affect my heart rate?",kw:['menstrual cycle','menstrual','menstruation','periods','period pain','period cramps','irregular periods','menopause','pms','ovulation'],
  p:["Yes. On average resting heart rate is slightly higher and HRV slightly lower in the second half of the cycle."],
  b:["Hot flushes and palpitations are common around menopause.","See a doctor for very heavy bleeding, severe pain or periods that become irregular."],rel:['hrvgood','pregnancy']},
 {id:'lowmood',q:"What should I do if I feel sad or low?",kw:['feel sad','feeling sad','low mood','feeling low','lonely','loneliness','grief','crying','hopeless','depressed','depression','sad'],
  p:["Feeling low happens to everyone. Talking to someone you trust, a daily routine, sunlight, movement and good sleep all help."],
  b:["If low mood lasts more than two weeks or affects work, sleep or relationships, speak to a doctor or counsellor.","If you ever think about harming yourself, contact your local emergency number or a crisis helpline right away."],rel:['stressmgmt','sleep']},
 {id:'panic',q:"What should I do during a panic attack?",kw:['panic attack','panic attacks','panic','hyperventilating','hyperventilation'],
  p:["A panic attack peaks within minutes and passes. It can feel like a heart attack, so get checked if it is your first one or you have chest pain."],
  b:["Breathe in for 4 and out for 6, and let the shoulders drop.","Try grounding: name 5 things you see, 4 you feel, 3 you hear, 2 you smell and 1 you taste.","Remind yourself it will pass."],w:true,rel:['breathtech','stressmgmt']},
 {id:'pollution',q:"How does air pollution affect my health?",kw:['air pollution','aqi','smog','pollution','n95 mask','air quality','air purifier'],
  p:["Polluted air can raise heart rate, lower HRV and worsen asthma, lung and heart conditions."],
  b:["On high-AQI days, limit outdoor exercise and keep windows closed.","A well-fitting N95 or FFP2 mask and an indoor air purifier help.","Take extra care if you have asthma, heart disease, are pregnant, elderly or a child."],rel:['asthma','hearthealth']},
 {id:'smartwatch',q:"Is a smartwatch better than this sensor?",kw:['smartwatch','fitbit','apple watch','garmin','wearable','oura','smart ring','mi band','smart band','fitness band'],
  p:["They suit different jobs. A watch tracks all day and night, which is great for trends. PulseSpace is a spot check with a finger sensor, which is often steadier for a quick pulse reading."],
  b:["Neither is a medical device.","Wrist sensors are less reliable during movement."],rel:['meddevice','spoacc']},
 {id:'hardware',q:"Can I use a different sensor?",kw:['different sensor','max30100','max30102','max30105','pulse sensor','ky 039','heart sensor','which sensor','other sensor','sensor model'],
  p:["The PulseSpace sketch is written for the MAX30102 and MAX30105. A MAX30100 has different settings and needs code changes."],
  b:["Simple analog pulse sensors have no red light, so they cannot estimate SpO₂.","If you change the sensor, the sketch needs to match it."],rel:['wiring']},
 {id:'wiring',q:"How do I wire the sensor to Arduino?",kw:['wiring','wire','pins','sda','scl','i2c','esp32','arduino uno','arduino nano','connections','circuit diagram','which pins'],
  p:["For an Uno or Nano:"],
  b:["VIN to 3.3 V, GND to GND, SDA to A4 and SCL to A5.","On an ESP32 use SDA 21 and SCL 22. On a Mega use SDA 20 and SCL 21.","Install the SparkFun MAX3010x library, upload the sketch and use a baud rate of 115200."],rel:['trouble','hardware']},
 {id:'energy',q:"How can I get more energy?",kw:['more energy','boost energy','energy levels','low stamina','stamina','feel energetic'],
  p:["Energy comes mostly from sleep, movement and steady eating."],
  b:["Get seven to nine hours of sleep and morning daylight.","Walk for ten minutes, drink water and eat protein with meals.","If tiredness lasts more than two weeks, ask about iron, B12 and thyroid tests."],rel:['fatigue','sleep']},
 {id:'focus',q:"How can I improve my focus and memory?",kw:['focus','concentration','memory','brain fog','concentrate','forgetful'],
  p:["Sleep, exercise and breaks matter most. Even a short walk improves attention."],
  b:["Work in 25 to 50 minute blocks with short breaks.","Cut multitasking, stay hydrated and limit late caffeine.","See a doctor if memory loss is sudden or worrying."],rel:['sleep','energy']},
 {id:'routine',q:"What is a healthy morning routine?",kw:['morning routine','daily routine','healthy routine','habits','healthy habits','lifestyle'],
  p:["A simple routine goes a long way:"],
  b:["Wake at the same time, drink water and get daylight.","Move for ten minutes and eat a protein-rich breakfast.","Measure with PulseSpace before coffee for the most consistent readings."],rel:['whenmeasure','exercise']}
);
