E('sigh',"What is a physiological sigh?",['physiological sigh','double inhale','double sigh','sigh breathing','huberman','quick calm breath','fastest way to calm down','calm down in seconds','calm in 30 seconds'],
 ["The physiological sigh is two quick breaths in through the nose, one on top of the other, then a long slow breath out through the mouth. It is one of the fastest ways to settle the body."],
 ["Do 2 or 3 rounds, then breathe normally.","The second sniff re-inflates collapsed air sacs and the long exhale slows the heart.","Stop if you feel lightheaded."],['breathtech','stresstools']);
E('pmr',"What is progressive muscle relaxation?",['progressive muscle relaxation','muscle relaxation','relax muscles','tense and release','jacobson','tense muscles','tight shoulders stress','jaw clenching','jaw tension','clenching teeth','shoulder tension stress'],
 ["Progressive muscle relaxation means tensing a muscle group for about 5 seconds, then releasing for 20 to 30 seconds, moving through the body."],
 ["Order: hands, forearms, upper arms, shoulders, face, jaw, chest, stomach, legs, feet.","Breathe out as you release.","Practise daily for 10 minutes and it works faster over time.","Skip any area that is injured or painful."],['stresstools','sleephygiene']);
E('grounding',"How does the 5-4-3-2-1 grounding technique work?",['5 4 3 2 1','54321','grounding technique','grounding exercise','ground myself','grounding anxiety','feel present','dissociation','spiralling','spiraling','racing thoughts','overwhelmed thoughts','stop overthinking','mind racing','cant stop thinking'],
 ["Grounding pulls your attention to the present through the senses: name 5 things you see, 4 you can touch, 3 you hear, 2 you smell and 1 you taste."],
 ["Say them out loud or silently, and go slowly.","Holding something cold or pressing your feet into the floor also helps.","If thoughts keep spiralling for weeks, talk to a doctor or counsellor."],['panic','anxietysym','stresstools']);
E('worrywindow',"How do I stop worrying so much?",['stop worrying','worry too much','worry window','constant worry','chronic worry','worry time','ruminating','rumination','overthinking','overthink','what if thoughts','catastrophizing'],
 ["Try a worry window: set aside 15 minutes a day for worries. When one pops up at other times, jot it down and tell yourself it is for later."],
 ["Write the worry and one possible next step. If nothing can be done, say so.","Move your body, since worry feeds on stillness.","Persistent worry that affects sleep or work is worth discussing with a doctor."],['anxietysym','stresstools']);
E('stresstools',"Give me a stress-control toolkit",['stress control','control stress','tips to reduce stress','reduce stress','reduce my stress','lower stress','stress tips','stress suggestions','stress management tips','stress ideas','stress advice','ways to reduce stress','ways to relax','how to relax','relax quickly','relax','feel calmer','calm down','stress relief','stress relievers','stress busting','destress','de stress','relieve stress','beat stress','handle stress','cope with stress','stress coping','coping skills','calm my mind','calm mind','reduce tension','manage tension'],
 ["Stress control works best when you use a few tools in layers: a fast one for the moment, a daily one for the baseline and a lifestyle one for the long term."],
 ["In the moment: physiological sigh, 4-in 6-out breathing, 5-4-3-2-1 grounding, a splash of cool water on the face.","Daily: 10 minutes of walking, 5 minutes of slow breathing, writing down worries, a real lunch break.","Long term: regular sleep, exercise, less caffeine and alcohol, time with people, and counselling if stress lingers.","Open the Tips toolkit on the Suggestions page, or start a Calm session, and watch your heart rate settle."],['sigh','breathtech','stressmgmt']);
E('stresssigns',"What are the signs of stress?",['signs of stress','stress symptoms','symptoms of stress','am i stressed','how do i know if im stressed','stress warning signs','stress physical symptoms','stress effects body','stress headache','stress stomach','tension headache'],
 ["Stress shows up in the body and mind: tight muscles, headaches, a faster pulse, poor sleep, stomach upset, irritability, trouble concentrating and low energy."],
 ["On PulseSpace, stress often shows as higher resting heart rate and lower HRV.","If signs last more than a couple of weeks, talk to a doctor."],['stressmgmt','stressheart','hrvgood']);
E('stressscorehigh',"My stress score is high. What should I do?",['stress score high','high stress score','stress score 80','stress score 70','stress is high','stress score elevated','why is my stress high','lower stress score','improve stress score','reduce stress score','stress 75','stress 90'],
 ["A high stress score on PulseSpace means your pulse is fast, your HRV is low, or both. It reflects your body's load right now, not a diagnosis."],
 ["Sit down, drink water and do 3 minutes of slow breathing, then measure again.","Check for caffeine, poor sleep, a heavy meal or a recent workout.","If it stays high for days, rest more and see a doctor if you feel unwell."],['stresstools','hrvimprove','sigh']);
E('stresssleep',"How does stress affect sleep?",['stress and sleep','cant sleep stress','stress insomnia','anxious at night','night anxiety','racing mind at night','worry at bedtime','sleep and anxiety','wake up anxious','3am wake','waking at 3am','early morning waking'],
 ["Stress keeps the body alert, which makes it harder to fall asleep and stay asleep. Poor sleep then raises stress, so the cycle feeds itself."],
 ["Write tomorrow's tasks before bed and do a slow breathing routine.","Keep the same bed and wake times.","Use 4-7-8 breathing in the Calm session.","Waking early with low mood for weeks needs a doctor."],['sleephygiene','insomnia']);
E('stresseating',"Why do I eat more when stressed?",['stress eating','emotional eating','eat when stressed','binge eating stress','comfort food stress','craving sweets stress','cortisol cravings','boredom eating','eating when anxious','loss of appetite stress'],
 ["Stress hormones can drive cravings for sugar and fat, and eating is a quick comfort. Some people lose their appetite instead."],
 ["Pause for 10 minutes and drink water or walk.","Keep fruit, nuts and curd handy and eat regular meals.","If it feels out of control, speak to a doctor or counsellor."],['stressfoods','snackideas']);
E('stressskin',"Can stress affect skin, hair and digestion?",['stress and hair loss','stress acne','stress skin','stress and digestion','stress ibs','stress ulcer','stress and periods','stress and immunity','stress cold sores','stress rash','stress and weight'],
